#Step 1: RepeatMasker
#Identifies and soft-masks transposable elements and repetitive sequences in accessory contigs,
#allowing downstream gene prediction (Augustus) and homology searches to focus on genuine
#coding sequence.
#
#Two rounds of masking performed:
#
#Round 1: Heliconius melpomene repeat library (Hmel2, ~2012-2014)
#         - Closest available Lepidoptera library on duke at time of first run
#         - Results: ~2.25% masking, but TE families misclassified (defaulted to homo taxonomy)
#         - Results in: repeatmasker_output/
#         - Not optimal I searched around a bit on duke at first and this was the one I at first was most optimal as a library..
#
#Round 2: Dfam Arthropoda library (Dfam v3.0, built into RepeatMasker), found this online and seemed like a much better choice than what the AI suggested.
#         - Following Berner et al. 2023 (Pieris mannii genome paper):
#         - Same approach for sister species P.mannii and P.rapae
#         - When doing it for C13STmor, I saw anmprovement over Round 1: 2.65% masking, TE families correctly classified
#           as insect-specific (LINEs, LTR elements, DNA transposons)
#         - Unclassified repeats dropped from 0.63% to 0.17%
#         - Species correctly set to arthropoda (not homo), which it was for some reason when I used Heliconius library.
#         - Results in: repeatmasker_output_arthropoda/ 
#
#Note on de novo library:
#         RepeatModeler v2.0.1 used by Berner et al. 2023 could not be installed on duke
#         due to dependency conflicts (rmblast, perl-text-soundex incompatible with system).
#         RepeatModeler v1.0.8 (installed on duke) failed with known RECON module bug
